[THOUGHT]  
The patient presents with marked, isolated lymphocytosis (absolute lymphocyte count 50,000/mm³) in the absence of B symptoms, organomegaly, or lymphadenopathy. This degree of lymphocytosis — especially when persistent and clonal — is highly characteristic of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL), the most common adult leukemia in Western countries. While other chronic lymphoproliferative disorders (e.g., monoclonal B-cell lymphocytosis [MBL], mantle cell lymphoma, or hairy cell leukemia) must be ruled out, CLL is by far the most likely diagnosis given:  
- Typical age group (though not specified, CLL is rare <40 years; clinical context implies older adult);  
- Absence of systemic symptoms or lymphoproliferative signs (favoring early-stage or asymptomatic CLL over aggressive lymphomas);  
- The threshold for CLL diagnosis is ≥5,000/μL clonal B-lymphocytes persisting ≥3 months — this patient exceeds that by an order of magnitude;  
- Flow cytometry (pending) is expected to show the classic immunophenotype: CD5+, CD23+, CD20(dim), sIg(dim), CD43+, FMC7–, with light-chain restriction — confirming clonality and supporting CLL over reactive lymphocytosis or other malignancies.  
Reactive causes (e.g., viral infection, autoimmune disease) are unlikely given the magnitude of lymphocytosis and lack of clinical correlates (no fever, no lymphadenopathy, no serologic evidence of infection). MBL would present with lower counts (<5,000/μL) and is a precursor state — this count is diagnostic-level.  

[DIAGNOSIS]  
Chronic lymphocytic leukemia

